/**

@page benchmark_multigrid_petsc_hdg_discretisation Hybridised DG for non-mathematicians

Here we discuss the Hybridised Discontinuous Galerkin formulation. We aim to explain a few things in
this page:

1. There are a number of degrees of freedom inside each cell, as well as on each face. Where
do these come from, and what fixes the number of them?

2. How do these aforementioned degrees of freedom interact with one another?

3. How do we construct our problem matrix @f$ A @f$ and our right-hand-side @f$ u @f$?

At time of writing, this document is being typed up based on discussion between TW and SB. This document
will be, for at least much of July 2023, highly variable whilst details are ironed out. This message
should be gone by the time there is some nice working code available.

Our aim, once we clarify all of the mathematics within this setup, is to describe the machinery
that will go into place behind the scenes. Compared to the continuous Galerkin tutorial
(\ref multigrid_continuous_galerkin_tutorial), this tutorial will be a blend of theory and
implementation.
Let's begin.

# How does this differ from the continuous case?

Before, we required that our test functions were linear. See \ref multigrid_continuous_galerkin_tutorial. 
Now, we drop this requirement. There's every chance we could get a more accurate solution
by using piecewise functions that don't "connect" with each other.

We now have twice as many degrees of freedom to fix: in our situation before, each test 
function neatly spanned two cells. **Now we have a slightly different scenario**

Some formulation
======================

As ever, we aim to solve:

@f$ - \nabla ^2 u = - \partial_x^2 u - \partial_y^2 u = f @f$

which is a second-order equation. Let us first introduce a helper variable @f$ \phi @f$, so we can instead 
write in a first-order formulation:

@f$ \phi_x = \partial_x u, \ \phi_y = \partial_y u @f$. 

We note that @f$ \phi @f$ is a @f$ d @f$-dimensional vector, which contains the gradient of @f$ u @f$ in each dimension.

This allows us to rewrite in first order, noting that the left hand side is just a divergence:

@f$ - \partial_x \phi_x - \partial_y \phi_y = f @f$ \n
@f$ - \text{div} \ \phi = f @f$.

## A note on the number of degrees of freedom

The DG formulation means that we have, for a given polynomial degree @f$ n @f$, and spatial dimension @f$ d@f$:

\li @f$ (n+1)^{d} @f$ degrees of freedom per cell

We note that @f$ (n+1)^{d-1} @f$ Dofs will reside on each face.
We arrange these points spatially in a Gauss-Lobatto pattern for the duration of this tutorial.

@image html gaussLobatto.png

We then aim to solve @f$ d+1 @f$ equations for each of these points:

\li We fix @f$ -\text{div} \ \phi = f @f$ at each of these points
\li We further fix  @f$ \partial_i u = \phi_i @f$ for each spatial dimension  @f$ i @f$.



## Let's integrate 

Since we have a first order formulation, we can simply integrate. We take the inner product with a test 
function @f$ \psi @f$, and then integrate over the cell:

@f$ \int_\text{cell} ( -\text{div} \ \phi, \psi ) dxdy = \int_\text{cell} ( f, \psi ) dxdy @f$.

Obviously, we will want to integrate over the whole domain. But for now we just consider this 
integral in each cell, as we can sum over them eventually.

The test functions we use are piecewise linear, scaled such that @f$ \psi_i @f$ is 1 right above @f$ u_i @f$ and 0 elsewhere

*THIS DOESN'T SQUARE WITH THERE BEING TWICE AS MANY TEST FUNCTIONS AS BEFORE. FIX THIS*

Next, we want to just consider the left-hand integral. Let's integrate by parts:

\f{eqnarray*}{
      \int_\text{cell} ( -\text{div} \ \phi, \psi ) dxdy &=& \int_\text{cell} ( \phi, \text{div} \ \psi ) dxdy - \int_\text{cell} \text{div} ( \phi \psi ) dx dy \ \ \ \forall \psi \\ 

       &=& \int_\text{cell} ( \phi, \text{div} \ \psi ) dxdy - \int_{\partial c} \phi \cdot \hat{n} \ \psi dS(x) \ \ \ \forall \psi
\f}

where in the second line we use Gauss divergence theorem to turn the second integral into an integral over the 
boundary of the cell (in other words over the face).

We note crucially that @f$ \phi @f$ does not exist on the face. This is because our approximate solution to @f$ u @f$ is discontinuous,
meaning we have a jump in the gradient. This is where we begin to diverge from the calculations 
demonstrated in \ref multigrid_continuous_galerkin_tutorial.
So, we introduce a new variable @f$ \hat{\phi} :=  [[ \phi \cdot \hat{n} ]]@f$, which is the
average of @f$ \phi @f$ in the left cell and the right cell. Below is a simplistic depiction, with a 1D cell.

@image html phiHat.png

Having introduced this, we can rewrite the right-hand integral as:

\f{eqnarray*}{
      &\approx& \int_\text{cell} ( \phi, \nabla \psi ) \ dxdy - \int_\text{face} [[ \phi \cdot \hat{n} ]] \cdot \psi \ dS(x)
\f}

Let's now discuss how we update @f$ u @f$ every step, using this formulation.

## How do we choose the basis functions this time?

Again, we have piecewise linear functions, but this time they are no longer continuous.
See the sketch below.

@image html testFunctionDG.png

## The Face Integral


If we solve our equation correctly, then our @f$ \hat{\phi} @f$ should end up in the middle. We think of this as another constraint we are placing on the solution.
We note also that when we enter each cell to perform some solver steps, we already
know our approximate solution @f$ u @f$, meaning we can drop the use of our helper
variable @f$ \phi @f$. Our solution will rest on correctly updating @f$ u @f$ and 
@f$ \hat{\phi} @f$


Let's now work out the contribution to the problem matrix that comes from this term. Let's now move to a 2D cell, where we have degree 1 Gauss-Lobatto points. This means we have 2 degrees of freedom on each face (which coincide spatially with the vertices), as well as 4 degrees of freedom on each cell (which again coincide with each vertex). This can be seen in page \ref multigrid_enumeration. Note that the vertices 
labelled (clockwise from bottom left) as 0, 2, 3, 1, which is an enumeration we keep throughout.

We aim to fix values of our approximation @f$ u @f$ at each of these spatial points, which we refer to as 
@f$ u^0, \ u^1, \ u^2, \ u^3 @f$. We will have also a value for @f$ \phi @f$ at each of these points too.

@image html cellFlat.png

So, let's first project the x component of the gradient onto face 0. We only keep components from 
vertex 0 and 2:

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \frac{1}{2}
  \begin{pmatrix}
  1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
  0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\
  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  \phi_x^0 \\
  \phi_x^1 \\
  \phi_x^2 \\
  \phi_x^3 \\
  \end{pmatrix}
\f}

We take a factor of a half here because we will perform this projection in an adjacent cell also.

We note further that the @f$ \phi @f$ variable only packages up the gradients of @f$ u @f$ at certain points. We note that, for instance, @f$ \phi_x^0 = (u^1 - u^0)/h @f$, since we want to calculate the gradient
in the x direction at point @f$ u^0 @f$; @f$ u^0 @f$ and @f$ u^1 @f$ are the only points which are somehow
"connected" in this axis, along this face.
Hence we can even rewrite the 
above equation as:

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \frac{1}{2}
  \begin{pmatrix}
  -1/h & 1/h & 0 & 0 \\
   0 & 0 & -1/h & 1/h  \\
  -1/h & 1/h & 0 & 0 \\
   0 & 0 & -1/h & 1/h  \\

  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
  0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\
  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  \phi_x^0 \\
  \phi_x^1 \\
  \phi_x^2 \\
  \phi_x^3 \\
  \end{pmatrix}
\f}

which is why we feel confident enough to kick out our helper variable @f$ \phi @f$; we can always reconstruct 
it on-the-fly.

TEST FUNCTION ARE RITZ GALERKIN. TAKEN FROM SAME FUNCTION SPACE WE HAVE THE u solution


## The cell integral

The first term tells us about the cell-cell coupling, and the second tells us about cell-face and face-cell. This is an integral over the whole cell, which we shall take as being 2D for this demo.

<!--
Let's first note what the gradient along the @f$ x @f$ direction is along the bottom face. At the 
endpoint, we have the value @f$ u^1 @f$, and at the start we have @f$ u^0 @f$. Hence the gradient along
this face is simply @f$ (u^1 - u^0) / h @f$.

So then, to work out the values of @f$ \phi @f$ along the cell, we must linearly interpolate between
the value of the gradient on the bottom face and the value at the top face. Hence we evaluate:


\f{eqnarray*}{

  \int_\text{cell}
  \begin{pmatrix}
  (u^1 - u^0) / h \cdot y + (u^3 - u^2) / h \cdot (1 - y) \\

  \end{pmatrix}

  \cdot 

  \begin{pmatrix}
  (0 - 1) / h \cdot y + (0 - 0) / h \cdot (1 - y) \\

  \end{pmatrix}

