Let's revisit the cell integral. What we are really trying to evaluate is:

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \int_\text{cell} (\phi, \ \text{div}\psi) dx \ \ \forall \psi

\f}

so that we can construct the problem matrix. This follows on from the continuous Galerkin
tutorial, but we put in some implementation details here.

### In theory

Here we basically repeat what was in the last chapter. Make the change from 
@f$ u @f$ to @f$ \sum_i u_i \psi_i @f$, and then write down what the matrix elements should be.


### In practice

**include a note about how we need to fetch global indices first**

Recall that @f$ \phi \ = \ \nabla u \ = \ (\partial_x u, \ \partial_y u )@f$. 
We demonstrate here how we will construct the matrix elements and/or stencil 
when we evaluate over one cell in particular. So, taking our cell (pictured above), with 
4 unknowns, we can first construct @f$ \phi @f$ at every point.

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \phi_x^0 \ = \ \phi_x^1 &=& \ \frac{u^1 - u^0}{h}\\
  \phi_x^2 \ = \ \phi_x^3 &=& \ \frac{u^3 - u^2}{h}\\
\f}

where we used the fact that our approximation is piecewise linear, and so the gradient
along each face is the same. 

<--!

We can do the same for the y-direction:

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \phi_y^0 \ = \ \phi_y^2 &=& \ \frac{u^2 - u^0}{h}\\
  \phi_y^1 \ = \ \phi_y^3 &=& \ \frac{u^3 - u^1}{h}\\

\f}

TECHNICALLY CORRECT - WE CAN DO THIS IN THE Y DIRECTION. BUT INSTEAD 
WE WILL WORK IT OUT ON THE BOTTOM FACE AND TOP FACE,
THEN INTERPOLATE ACROSS

